# OData $batch demonstration.
#
# OData lets you pack multiple operations into a single HTTP round-trip via POST /odata/$batch.
# Each sub-request is routed and executed as if it had been sent individually. The response body
# contains one entry per sub-request, in the same order, each with its own status + body. Sub-requests
# can declare dependencies via an "id" + "dependsOn" — the server enforces the order so a CREATE can
# be referenced by a follow-up GET in the same batch.
#
# Two formats supported:
#   1. application/json — modern, easy to read, recommended (used here)
#   2. multipart/mixed — original OData v3 wire format, still works
#
# Server wiring:
#   - DefaultODataBatchHandler passed to AddRouteComponents in ProgramExtensionsCustom
#   - app.UseODataBatching() before app.UseRouting in the pipeline
@host = 127.0.01:443
@batchUrl = https://{{host}}/odata/$batch

###
# @name JsonBatch_CreateChef_CreateRecipe_GetRecipe
# Demonstrates inter-request dependencies. Operation r2 (POST Recipe) depends on r1 (POST Chef)
# because the Chef must exist before the Recipe FK can resolve. Operation r3 (GET Recipe) depends on
# r2. The server runs them in dependency order; if any earlier op fails, dependents short-circuit.
POST {{batchUrl}} HTTP/1.1
Host: {{host}}
Content-Type: application/json
Cache-Control: no-cache

{
	"requests": [
		{
			"id": "r1",
			"method": "POST",
			"url": "/odata/Chef",
			"headers": { "content-type": "application/json" },
			"body": { "Name": "Batch Tester", "Url": "https://batch.test/" }
		},
		{
			"id": "r2",
			"method": "POST",
			"url": "/odata/Recipe",
			"headers": { "content-type": "application/json" },
			"dependsOn": ["r1"],
			"body": {
				"ChefId": 1,
				"Name": "Batch Recipe",
				"Url": "https://batch.test/r1",
				"Yield": 4,
				"GramPerYield": 350,
				"CategoryCode": null
			}
		},
		{
			"id": "r3",
			"method": "GET",
			"url": "/odata/Recipe/1?$expand=Chef",
			"dependsOn": ["r2"]
		}
	]
}

###
# @name JsonBatch_ParallelReads
# Independent reads — no dependsOn means the server may execute them in parallel.
# Useful for fanning out queries against multiple entity sets in a single round-trip.
POST {{batchUrl}} HTTP/1.1
Host: {{host}}
Content-Type: application/json
Cache-Control: no-cache

{
	"requests": [
		{ "id": "chefs",      "method": "GET", "url": "/odata/Chef?$top=5&$select=Id,Name" },
		{ "id": "recipes",    "method": "GET", "url": "/odata/Recipe?$top=5&$select=Id,Name" },
		{ "id": "categories", "method": "GET", "url": "/odata/CategoryCode?$top=5" }
	]
}

###
# @name MultipartBatch_OriginalFormat
# Same idea as the JSON batch, but using OData's original multipart/mixed wire format.
# Some legacy OData clients only speak this — the server accepts both.
POST {{batchUrl}} HTTP/1.1
Host: {{host}}
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=batch_36522ad7-fc75-4b56-8c71-56071383e77b
Cache-Control: no-cache

--batch_36522ad7-fc75-4b56-8c71-56071383e77b
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

GET /odata/Chef?$top=3 HTTP/1.1
Host: {{host}}

--batch_36522ad7-fc75-4b56-8c71-56071383e77b
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

GET /odata/Recipe?$top=3 HTTP/1.1
Host: {{host}}

--batch_36522ad7-fc75-4b56-8c71-56071383e77b--
